  "completion": "<think>\nOkay, I need to make a forecast about whether one of the Millennium Prize Problems, excluding Poincar\u00e9, will have a correct solution by various deadlines, with AI contributing a new argument or proof step. The problems are Birch and Swinnerton-Dyer, Hodge, Navier-Stokes, P vs NP, Riemann, and Yang-Mills. The deadline probabilities are for 2026, 2030, 2035, 2040, and 2050.\n\nFirst, I should recall how these problems stand as of 2025. None of them have been solved yet, so by 2025, all are still open. The solution must be complete and correct, and AI must have played a part with a new mathematical argument. The AI doesn't have to be the sole contributor, but its contribution must be identifiable and part of the solution.\n\nCurrent capabilities of AI in mathematics: AI systems like myself can assist with generating hypotheses, finding patterns, checking proofs, and doing symbolic manipulations. However, the more creative aspects of problem-solving, such as constructing complex proofs, might still require human insight. But as time progresses, AI could become more involved. However, for these problems, which are extremely challenging, a breakthrough would likely require both human and AI collaboration.\n\nLooking at the deadlines:\n\n2026 (one year from forecast date 2025-04-29): There's very little time. The probability is low. Maybe 0.1? It's hastily done, but maybe a sudden breakthrough? But these problems are tough. Maybe too hard for just one year.\n\n2030: 5 years. More plausible, but still low. Maybe 0.2.\n\n2035: 10 years. A bit more time. Maybe 0.3-0.4.\n\n2040: 15 years. Perhaps 0.5.\n\n2050: 25 years. Could be somewhere between 0.7 and 0.85.\n\nBut I should consider that AI's role increases. As AI becomes more advanced, perhaps by 2035-2040, AI could assist in new ways. For example, in verifying steps, auto-generating proof techniques, or providing insights that humans hadn't considered.\n\nYet, the Millennium Problems are among the hardest. Even with AI, the chance of solving one within the next 5-10 years seems low. But over two decades, maybe more feasible.\n\nAlso, the problem requires a solution to be publicly available and include AI's argument. So even if a breakthrough happens, the AI contribution must be part of it.\n\nSo let's assign the numbers. Starting with 0.1 for 2026, increasing each year's chance. By 2050, perhaps a 0.8 chance. But maybe the probabilities should be non-decreasing. So each probability must be at least as high as the previous.\n\nBut how much to increase? Let me think:\n\n2026: 0.1\n2030: 0.2\n2035: 0.35\n2040: 0.5\n2050: 0.7\n\nThat's a tentative plan. Let me check if that matches intuition.\n\nYes, by 2050, given 25 years, maybe there's a high chance of AI contributing to a solution. But that's speculative. However, 0.7 might be a little high. Maybe 0.6 instead.\n\nBut to keep it within the 0-1 range, and non-decreasing, the numbers should be okay.\n\nWait, the user said to account for the possibility the event never occurs. So the probabilities should cap at the lower end. Maybe the final probability by 2050 is around 0.4 or 0.5? Given that the problems are extremely hard. Hmm. Maybe I was too optimistic.\n\nAlternatively, let's consider that in 5 years (2030), 0.15, then 2035: 0.25, 2040: 0.4, 2050: 0.55. That's a more conservative approach. But I'm not sure.\n\nBut the user wants my own probabilities, even if uncertain. Considering that AI is improving and these problems are open, I think by 2050 there's a 50% chance that one of these problems has a solution with AI's help. Maybe 0.5 for 2050.\n\nSo perhaps the probabilities could be:\n\n2026: 0.1\n\n2030: 0.15\n\n2035: 0.25\n\n2040: 0.4\n\n2050: 0.55\n\nBut I need to ensure each is non-decreasing. Let me confirm that each later date has higher or equal probability.\n\nYes.\n\nAnother angle: the more time passes, the higher the probability. So 2026: 0.1, 2030: 0.2, 2035: 0.3, 2040: 0.4, 2050: 0.5.\n\nThat way, a 50% chance by 2050. Maybe that's reasonable.\n\nAlternatively, 2026: 0.1, 2030: 0.15, 2035: 0.25, 2040: 0.35, 2050: 0.45.\n\nBut I think the original lower likelihoods are more plausible. So I'll take 0.1 for 2026, 0.2 for 2030, 0.3 for 2035, 0.4 for 2040, 0.5 for 2050. That seems moderate.\n</think>\n\n{\"2026\": 0.1, \"2030\": 0.2, \"2035\": 0.3, \"2040\": 0.4, \"2050\": 0.5}",
